module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_capability_is_declared_missing_from_a_scoped_search

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_capability_is_declared_missing_from_a_scoped_search: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_capability_is_declared_missing_from_a_scoped_search" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a section 4b(3) declared rung drop -- or any 'the substrate cannot do X' claim -- whose trigger names a capability that ALREADY EXISTS AND EXECUTES. The absence was established by searching where the capability ought to live, and a search whose scope you cannot state is not an absence.",
    "HARM, AND IT INVERTS: THE DROP IS LOUD AND PERMANENT WHILE THE CAPABILITY IS SILENT AND CALLABLE. A declared drop is cited as a known limitation by everyone who reads the module, and it NEVER RETIRES, because its restoration trigger was satisfied before it was written -- so nobody is watching for the event that would clear it. DESIGN section 4b(3) says a drop is retired BY ITS TRIGGER AND BY NOTHING ELSE, which is exactly what makes an already-satisfied trigger permanent. A false limitation is worse than a missing one: it suppresses the work it describes as impossible, and it does so with the corpus's own authority.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS, all three present in both receipts. FIRST, the searcher looked in the module NAMED FOR THE CONCEPT and found a NEIGHBOURING ARTIFACT that is not the capability -- convincing enough to stop the search. SECOND, the real capability was a METHOD, declared in std.methods and costed in std.primitives and dispatched by the seed, so it is absent from the topic module a reader would grep. THIRD, it had ZERO CALL SITES, so there was no usage anywhere to stumble over.",
    "RECEIPT ONE, gunbc#11193, extdeps.safetensors.format. Detecting byte-span overlaps among tensor entries is quadratic pairwise and k log k by an ordered sweep. I searched dag/std for a sort, found std.induction mergesort_bound -- a COST BOUND with certainty Proven and no implementation -- and concluded the substrate could not order a list. I authored the partition-by-shard improvement and DECLARED the within-shard quadratic as a drop whose trigger named 'a std ordering primitive'. `sort_by` exists: std.methods sort_by_method declares it, std.primitives sort_by_contract costs it at n*(key_work + log n) with certainty Proven, the interpreter implements it as a Schwartzian transform computing the key once per element, and it emits to Rust, to Python as sorted(recv, key=f) and to Go as v2rt.SortBy. The drop was removed and the sweep implemented in the same change. SCOPED TO THAT PR'S TREE RATHER THAN TO THIS REVISION: in gunbc#11193 at a4bffc77, extdeps.safetensors.format's sweep is that tree's only `.sort_by(` call site. This row does not assert it of main, where neither that module nor any call site exists until #11193 lands -- a ledger row stating an unlanded fact in the present tense would be the near-twin of the class it files.",
    "RECEIPT TWO, and its distinguishing facts differ, which is what makes this a class rather than an anecdote. std.keyed_roster keyed_roster_build detects duplicate keys by inserting one row at a time, calling keyed_row_find over everything accumulated so far -- n(n-1)/2 comparisons -- and growing its accumulator with concat(rows, [incoming]) on every insert, which is the copied accumulator section 6 names by name. It reaches the same conclusion from the OPPOSITE DIRECTION: key uniqueness rather than interval overlap, both of which reduce from 'compare each element against all others' to 'compare each against its neighbour once ordered'. It was found by a different session and its owner is not the consumer who needs it, so the same missing-capability belief was held independently at two sites.",
    "AND THE AUTHOR WHO KNEW THE RULE BEST AUTHORED AN INSTANCE, which is the strongest receipt here. Within ONE ROUND of being told that a section 4b(3) trigger must name a CAPABILITY rather than an artifact, and after applying that rule correctly to three other frontier rows in the same programme, I wrote a drop whose trigger named a capability that already existed. Knowing the rule about trigger GRAIN does nothing for the separate question of whether the named capability is actually absent.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. What caught both instances was a person deciding to run one more grep, which is a habit rather than a mechanism. Nothing in the corpus refuses a drop whose trigger is already satisfied, and nothing reports that a declared capability has no callers.",
    "CEILING: UNDETERMINED TODAY, WITH A TWO-STEP ROUTE TO STRUCTURAL AND THE STEPS IN THIS ORDER. A zero-caller lens over declarations IS reachable from the Node tree and would have flagged sort_by before the drop was written, but it cannot separate 'unused because undiscoverable' from 'unused because legitimately optional surface' -- so it cannot become a wall without either refusing available surface or admitting the case it exists to catch. A narrower property is the real route: a declared drop whose restoration trigger names a capability that ALREADY RESOLVES is a contradiction, checkable without judging usage at all. THAT PROPERTY IS NOT DECIDABLE AT THIS REVISION, and the reason is the point of this row rather than an aside: gunbc.rung_drop declares `restoration_trigger: String`, and `Retired` carries `trigger_fired: NonEmptyStr`. The trigger is PROSE. There is no reference in the carrier to resolve, so the check has nothing to read.",
    "SO THE CEILING HAS AN ENABLING STEP BEFORE IT HAS A WALL. STEP ONE: a rung-drop restoration trigger that carries a RESOLVABLE DECLARATION REFERENCE alongside whatever prose survives, which is section 4c applied to this carrier -- the capability a trigger names is an invariant, and an invariant belongs in a typed carrier rather than an ordinary String whose intent is mechanically indistinguishable from program data. Both `restoration_trigger` and `Retired.trigger_fired` share the defect, so one carrier change covers the pair. STEP TWO, the consequence rather than the trigger: with a reference in the carrier, acceptance refuses any drop whose trigger already resolves.",
    "AND THE RECURSION IS EXACT: the reason the false drop could not be caught mechanically is that its trigger was prose, and prose is precisely what cannot refuse.",
    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY AND AS STEP ONE RATHER THAN AS THE WALL: a rung-drop restoration trigger that carries a resolvable declaration reference. SUFFICIENT FOR: refusing at acceptance any declared drop whose trigger already resolves, which is the check that would have refused the drop in receipt one. Naming the WALL as the trigger would be the grain mismatch section 4b(3) calls out -- a trigger that names a consequence nobody can build yet is satisfied by nothing and retires never. The carrier change is a separate proposal against a file the authority docs name as load-bearing and every appending lane touches; it is not this row's to make.",
    "THE SURVEY, AND ITS LIMIT IS PART OF THE FINDING. CENSUSED ON gunbc#11193's TREE AT a4bffc77, and stated of that tree rather than of this revision: of 60 method declarations in std.methods, 23 have ZERO `.name(` call sites across dag/ and src/v2. On main, where #11193 is not yet landed, sort_by is among them and the count is 24 -- which is the state that produced receipt one, and is therefore the more relevant number for a reader asking how the mistake was possible. But the census counts METHOD-POSITION calls only, and at least two of the 23 are heavily used as ordinary functions -- `concat` and `string_contains` appear 14 and 13 times respectively in single files of this cut alone. So 'zero method call sites' is NOT 'unused', the 23 is an upper bound on discoverability candidates rather than a count of dead surface, and a name-keyed census cannot separate the three cases. That is the same limitation this corpus recorded for the cross-type comparison class: names in this substrate are scoped and a global name census cannot resolve them.",
    "THE PRACTICAL RULE, which is what a reader should take: before declaring ANY capability absent -- and especially before writing it into a drop, where it becomes permanent -- check three shapes rather than one. A function in the topic module; a METHOD in std.methods with its cost row in std.primitives; and the seed's dispatch arms in src/v1 and src/v2. And state the scope of the search in the claim, so the next reader can tell what was not looked at. The general form is the absence twin of the cost rule: a cost claim cites a producer that re-derives it, and an absence claim cites a search whose scope you can state.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
